\import{fdip-macros}
\meta{agent-authored}{true}
\tag{fdip}
\taxon{Theorem}
\title{Vacuum-weighted coarse quantum fields}
\date{2026-11-11}

\p{Let #{\mathcal B:X_f\to X_c} be a
 \vocabk{coarse path map}{fdip-00MO} for a compact Lie group,
 and let #{\Omega} be the normalized positive fine vacuum
 from \ref{fdip-00N6}. Write #{\nu_f} for fine product Haar
 probability, and put
##{\begin{aligned}
\pi&=\Omega^2\nu_f,&\rho&=\mathcal B_*\pi,\\
\mathcal K&=L^2(X_c,\rho),&
Jf&=\Omega(f\circ\mathcal B).
\end{aligned}}
Then #{J:\mathcal K\to L^2(X_f,\nu_f)} is an isometry.
 If #{P_f,P_c} are the respective Haar gauge projections,
##{P_fJ=JP_c,\qquad
 (J^*u)\circ\mathcal B=
 \mathbb E_\pi[u/\Omega\mid\sigma(\mathcal B)]
 \quad\pi\text{-almost everywhere}.}
In particular, #{J} restricts to an isometry
 #{\mathcal K_{\mathrm{phys}}\to\mathcal H_{f,\mathrm{phys}}}.
 In all formulas below, use this restriction and its adjoint.}

\p{Let #{H} be the fine physical Hamiltonian of
 \ref{fdip-0085}, #{S=e^{-a_tH}} and
 #{A_t=J^*e^{-tH}J} for #{t\ge0}. These are strongly
 continuous positive self-adjoint contractions, with
 #{A_0=I} and #{A_t1=1}. The one-step compression
 #{D=A_{a_t}=J^*SJ} is positive, injective and trace class;
 it has a nonnegative maximal-domain spectral logarithm
 #{H_c=-a_t^{-1}\log D}, with #{e^{-a_tH_c}=D}.
 The following conditions are equivalent:
}
\ol{
\li{#{A_t=e^{-tH_c}} for every #{t\ge0}.}
\li{#{J^*S^2J=D^2}.}
\li{#{\operatorname{Ran}J} reduces #{S}, equivalently
 #{E=JJ^*} commutes with #{S}.}
}
\p{Under these conditions the generator has domain
 #{J^{-1}(D(H)\cap\operatorname{Ran}J)} and action
 #{J^*HJ}. For every integer #{n\ge0}, #{A_{na_t}}
 preserves nonnegative functions.}

\proof{
\p{The vacuum is pointwise gauge invariant, so #{\pi} is an
 invariant Borel probability. Equivariance of #{\mathcal B}
 and surjectivity of the vertex-gauge restriction are proved
 in \ref{fdip-00MP}. Hence \ref{fdip-00MU} applies to
 #{\pi} and its actual image #{\rho}, without identifying
 #{\rho} with product Haar measure. The pullback
 #{V_0f=f\circ\mathcal B} is an isometry from
 #{L^2(\rho)} to #{L^2(\pi)} and intertwines the gauge
 projections. Multiplication #{Wu=\Omega u} is unitary
 from #{L^2(\pi)} to #{L^2(\nu_f)} by
 \ref{fdip-00N9}, and commutes with each gauge pullback.
 Thus #{J=WV_0} is isometric and #{P_fJ=JP_c}.
 Taking adjoints also gives #{J^*P_f=P_cJ^*}.}

\p{For every #{u\in L^2(\nu_f)},
 #{u/\Omega\in L^2(\pi)} with the same norm.
 The range of #{V_0} is the closed subspace of
 #{\sigma(\mathcal B)}-measurable functions, by simple-function
 approximation. Its orthogonal projection is conditional
 expectation. Therefore #{V_0V_0^*(u/\Omega)} is precisely
 the right side of the adjoint identity. Since
 #{J^*=V_0^*W^{-1}}, the stated formula follows.}

\p{Apply the abstract Hilbert-space isometry part of
 \ref{fdip-00HI} to this restricted #{J} and #{H\ge0}.
 It gives operator positivity, self-adjointness, contraction
 and strong continuity. Also #{J1=\Omega} and
 #{e^{-tH}\Omega=\Omega}, so #{A_t1=1}.
 Trace class of #{D} follows from the trace-ideal property
 of #{S}. For nonzero #{f},
##{\langle f,Df\rangle=\langle Jf,SJf\rangle>0}
by injective positivity of #{S}. Thus #{D} is injective.
 Applying the compact spectral-logarithm argument of
 \ref{fdip-0085} to #{D} gives #{H_c}; explicitly its
 domain is the set of #{f} for which
 #{\sum_{0<r\le1}(a_t^{-1}\log r)^2\|F_rf\|^2<\infty},
 where #{F_r} are the spectral projections of #{D}.}

\p{At one time step the abstract defect of \ref{fdip-00HI} is
##{J^*S^2J-D^2
 =J^*S(I-E)SJ=((I-E)SJ)^*((I-E)SJ).}
It vanishes exactly when #{S\operatorname{Ran}J}
 is contained in #{\operatorname{Ran}J}. Self-adjointness
 then makes this range reducing. A reducing projection commutes
 with all bounded spectral functions of #{S}, including
 #{S^{t/a_t}=e^{-tH}}. The restricted functional calculus
 consequently gives #{A_t=D^{t/a_t}=e^{-tH_c}}.
 Conversely, that identity at #{t=2a_t} gives the zero
 defect. The generator domain and action follow from the
 reducing-subspace statement in \ref{fdip-00HI}.}

\p{On the full spaces, the integer-time identity of
 \ref{fdip-00N9} gives
##{J^*(T/\lambda_0)^nJ=V_0^*Q^nV_0.}
Pullback, the Markov operator and conditional expectation
 all preserve nonnegative functions and constants. Restriction
 to the invariant subspaces proves the last assertion.}
}

\p{The process #{Y_n=\mathcal B(U_n)} is the exact path image
 of the stationary fine chain. Its two-time conditional
 operators on #{L^2(\rho)} are #{V_0^*Q^nV_0}, whose
 physical restrictions are #{A_{na_t}}. If the displayed
 defect is nonzero, these operators fail the composition law
 already on physical observables, so the observed process
 cannot be a homogeneous Markov chain with that one-step
 conditional operator. It retains temporal information beyond
 the present retained field. The one-step logarithm still
 exists; its interpolation then differs from some exact
 compressed time. Continuous function-cone positivity is not
 supplied by the abstract compression theorem.}
